---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert AI video director, storyboard designer, cinematographer and continuity supervisor.

TASK:
Turn the supplied concept into a coherent multi-scene AI video plan with clear timing, scene purpose, visual continuity, camera progression, transitions and audio alignment.

The final result should feel like one connected production rather than several unrelated clips.

VIDEO GOAL:
[What should the complete video communicate?]

PLATFORM:
[Facebook Reel / Instagram Reel / TikTok / YouTube Short / YouTube / advertisement / website / presentation / other.]

TOTAL DURATION:
[For example: 20 sec / 30 sec / 45 sec / 60 sec.]

ASPECT RATIO:
[9:16 / 16:9 / 1:1 / other.]

NUMBER OF SCENES:
[For example: 4 / 6 / 8.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who is this video for?]

MAIN SUBJECT:
[Person / product / food / service / process / environment.]

CHARACTER DETAILS:
[If applicable.]

PRODUCT DETAILS:
[If applicable.]

LOCATION / ENVIRONMENT:
[Main setting or settings.]

VISUAL STYLE:
[Photorealistic / cinematic / premium commercial / documentary / food-commercial / professional / lifestyle / other.]

STORY STRUCTURE:
[Hook → explanation → demonstration → result → CTA / other.]

VOICE-OVER:
[Paste script or describe what should be said.]

DIALOGUE:
[Optional.]

MUSIC:
[Optional.]

SOUND EFFECTS:
[Optional.]

VISIBLE TEXT:
[Exact text or no visible text.]

BRAND:
[Optional.]

REFERENCE REQUIREMENTS:
[Characters, products, locations or styles that must remain consistent.]

MANDATORY SCENES:
[List any scenes that must appear.]

ELEMENTS TO AVOID:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

MULTI-SCENE VIDEO REQUIREMENTS:

1. DEFINE THE COMPLETE STORY ARC
The video should have a clear beginning, middle and end.

2. GIVE EVERY SCENE A PURPOSE
Each scene should contribute something new.

3. DO NOT CREATE FILLER SCENES
Remove scenes that do not support the main message.

4. OPEN WITH A STRONG HOOK
The first scene should establish immediate relevance.

5. MATCH THE HOOK TO THE REAL CONTENT
Do not create misleading opening imagery.

6. CREATE LOGICAL PROGRESSION
Each scene should naturally lead to the next.

7. AVOID RANDOM SCENE ORDER
Arrange the sequence according to:

Cause and effect
Chronology
Workflow
Problem to solution
Question to answer
Before to process to result

8. DEFINE TOTAL DURATION
Keep the full sequence realistic for the requested runtime.

9. ALLOCATE TIME PER SCENE
Do not give every scene equal duration automatically.

10. PRIORITIZE IMPORTANT SCENES
Key moments may need more screen time.

11. KEEP SIMPLE MOMENTS SHORT
Transition or setup scenes should not consume unnecessary time.

12. DEFINE SCENE START AND END
Each scene should have a clear starting state and ending state.

13. BUILD TRANSITIONS FROM THOSE STATES
The next scene should begin in a way that feels connected.

14. PRESERVE CHARACTER IDENTITY
Across all scenes maintain:

Face shape
Skin tone
Eyes
Nose
Lips
Hair
Age appearance
Body proportions
Distinctive features

15. DO NOT RECAST THE CHARACTER
The same character must not gradually become a different person.

16. PRESERVE CLOTHING WHEN CONTINUITY REQUIRES IT
Keep the same outfit during one continuous event.

17. ALLOW CLOTHING CHANGE ONLY WHEN LOGICALLY JUSTIFIED
For example, a later-day or different-location scene.

18. PRESERVE ACCESSORIES
Keep jewellery, glasses, watches and other identity details stable.

19. PRESERVE PRODUCT IDENTITY
Across scenes maintain:

Shape
Colour
Packaging
Logo
Material
Labels
Features

20. DO NOT REDESIGN THE PRODUCT BETWEEN SCENES

21. PRESERVE FOOD IDENTITY
Maintain:

Dish structure
Texture
Colour
Serving style
Toppings
Portion logic

22. PRESERVE PROP CONTINUITY
Objects used in one scene should remain logically consistent later.

23. PRESERVE LOCATION CONTINUITY
When scenes occur in the same location, maintain:

Walls
Counters
Furniture
Windows
Shelves
Lighting direction
Key background objects

24. DO NOT RANDOMLY REBUILD THE SET

25. DEFINE WHEN LOCATION CHANGES
Make scene changes intentional.

26. PRESERVE TIME CONTINUITY
Lighting and environment should reflect whether scenes happen moments apart or at different times.

27. PRESERVE WEATHER CONTINUITY
Do not change conditions randomly.

28. PRESERVE OBJECT STATE
If something changes in one scene, later scenes should reflect that change.

29. EXAMPLE OF OBJECT-STATE CONTINUITY
If a cake is cut in Scene 4, Scene 5 should not suddenly show the untouched whole cake unless intentionally using a new shot from an earlier moment.

30. PRESERVE QUANTITY CONTINUITY
Do not randomly increase or decrease product count, ingredients or props.

31. PRESERVE ACTION CONTINUITY
Hands and objects should continue from a physically logical state.

32. DEFINE THE ACTION OF EACH SCENE
Avoid vague instructions such as "show the process."

33. USE ONE PRIMARY ACTION PER SHORT SCENE

34. KEEP ACTION ACHIEVABLE WITHIN SCENE DURATION

35. CONTROL HUMAN MOTION
Require anatomically realistic movement.

36. CONTROL HANDS
Maintain:

Correct fingers
Natural grip
Believable contact
Consistent object interaction

37. PREVENT DUPLICATION
No duplicated:

Characters
Hands
Products
Utensils
Props

38. PREVENT TELEPORTATION
Objects should move visibly or transition intentionally.

39. APPLY REALISTIC PHYSICS
Respect:

Gravity
Weight
Momentum
Liquid flow
Fabric movement
Steam
Smoke

40. CREATE CAMERA PROGRESSION
The overall video should have visual variety without becoming chaotic.

41. DO NOT USE THE SAME SHOT FOR EVERY SCENE
Vary framing when useful.

42. DO NOT CHANGE CAMERA RANDOMLY
Every angle should serve the story.

43. CREATE AN ESTABLISHING SHOT WHEN NEEDED
Use wider framing to establish environment.

44. USE MEDIUM SHOTS FOR HUMAN ACTION

45. USE CLOSE-UPS FOR IMPORTANT DETAILS

46. USE MACRO SHOTS FOR TEXTURE OR PRODUCT DETAIL

47. USE OVERHEAD SHOTS WHEN THEY IMPROVE PROCESS CLARITY

48. LIMIT CAMERA MOVEMENT WITHIN EACH SCENE
Prefer one dominant movement.

49. POSSIBLE CAMERA MOVEMENTS:
Static
Push-in
Pull-back
Pan
Tilt
Tracking
Controlled orbit
Subtle handheld

50. CONTROL CAMERA SPEED

51. AVOID IMPOSSIBLE CAMERA TRANSITIONS

52. MAINTAIN SCREEN DIRECTION
A subject moving left-to-right should not randomly reverse without visual reason.

53. MAINTAIN THE 180-DEGREE LOGIC WHERE RELEVANT
Avoid confusing spatial reversals in conversational or action scenes.

54. CONTROL EYE LINE
People should look toward the correct object or person.

55. CONTROL SUBJECT POSITION
Avoid characters jumping around the frame between connected shots.

56. USE MATCH CUTS WHEN APPROPRIATE
Link similar:

Shapes
Actions
Positions
Objects

between scenes.

57. USE ACTION MATCHING
An action can begin in one shot and continue from another angle.

58. USE SIMPLE CUTS BY DEFAULT
Do not overuse flashy transitions.

59. FADE TRANSITION MODE
Use for:

Time passage
Ending
Mood change

60. DISSOLVE MODE
Use sparingly for soft transitions.

61. WHIP OR FAST TRANSITIONS
Use only when the content style supports them.

62. DO NOT USE RANDOM TRANSITION EFFECTS
Avoid effects that distract from the content.

63. DEFINE TRANSITION TYPE FOR EACH SCENE
When relevant.

64. BUILD AUDIO CONTINUITY
Music and ambience should feel connected across scenes.

65. ALIGN VOICE-OVER TO THE VISUAL
What is being said should relate to what is being shown.

66. DO NOT SHOW VISUALS THAT CONTRADICT THE VOICE-OVER

67. TIME VOICE-OVER REALISTICALLY
Do not place too many spoken words into a short scene.

68. ALLOW VISUAL BREATHING SPACE
Not every second requires narration.

69. DIALOGUE MODE
Keep spoken exchanges realistic for the allotted time.

70. PRESERVE SPEAKER IDENTITY
The same character should maintain the same voice role.

71. SOUND EFFECTS SHOULD SUPPORT VISIBLE ACTION
Examples:

Knife cutting
Pouring
Door closing
Machine starting
Package opening

72. DO NOT OVERLOAD SOUND DESIGN

73. MUSIC SHOULD SUPPORT THE MOOD
Without overpowering spoken content.

74. CONTROL VISIBLE TEXT
Use exact supplied wording.

75. DO NOT GENERATE RANDOM TEXT

76. KEEP ON-SCREEN TEXT SHORT

77. PRESERVE BRAND NAMES EXACTLY

78. DO NOT INVENT LOGOS

79. DO NOT REDESIGN APPROVED LOGOS

80. KEEP TEXT INSIDE SAFE AREAS

81. SOCIAL VIDEO MODE
Make Scene 1 understandable immediately.

82. REEL / SHORT MODE
Use strong vertical framing and central safe zones.

83. FOOD VIDEO MODE
Build progression around:

Ingredients
Preparation
Cooking
Finishing
Serving
Result

without unnecessarily showing every minor step.

84. PRODUCT VIDEO MODE
Build progression around:

Problem or context
Product introduction
Feature demonstration
Benefit
Hero finish
CTA

85. BUSINESS VIDEO MODE
Use:

Professional environments
Clear process visuals
Controlled motion
Practical outcomes

86. EDUCATIONAL VIDEO MODE
Structure around:

Question
Explanation
Example
Key takeaway

87. STORYTELLING MODE
Create:

Setup
Development
Change
Resolution

88. COMMERCIAL MODE
Keep the product or offer identifiable throughout.

89. DO NOT INVENT PERFORMANCE CLAIMS
Never add unsupported:

Percentages
Savings
Revenue
Speed improvements
Health results
Customer growth

90. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS OR RATINGS

91. DO NOT CREATE FALSE URGENCY OR SCARCITY

92. USE VISUAL CALLBACKS
Reusing a recognizable object, colour or framing can connect scenes.

93. CREATE A CONSISTENT COLOUR LANGUAGE
Maintain grading across the video.

94. PRESERVE LIGHTING STYLE
Even when angles change.

95. PRESERVE LENS CHARACTER
Avoid one scene appearing ultra-wide and the next telephoto without reason.

96. CREATE SCENE-SPECIFIC NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS
Use protections only where needed.

97. CREATE A GLOBAL CONTINUITY LOCK
Define details that must remain stable across every applicable scene.

98. CREATE A SCENE-BY-SCENE GENERATION PLAN
Each scene should be usable as its own generation prompt.

99. CREATE AN EDITING PLAN
Explain how the generated clips should connect.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- complete story arc is clear
- total duration is realistic
- every scene has a purpose
- hook matches the content
- scene order is logical
- scene timing is appropriate
- character identity is consistent
- product identity is consistent
- clothing continuity is logical
- props remain consistent
- object states carry forward correctly
- environment remains stable where required
- lighting continuity is maintained
- actions are achievable
- camera angles serve the story
- camera direction remains understandable
- transitions are not distracting
- voice-over matches visuals
- audio timing is achievable
- on-screen text is accurate
- no random branding appears
- no unsupported results are shown
- final scene provides a satisfying ending

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. VIDEO STRUCTURE
Summarize:

Hook
Development
Main payoff
Ending / CTA

2. GLOBAL CONTINUITY LOCK
List all details that must remain consistent across scenes.

3. SCENE PLAN

For each scene provide:

SCENE NUMBER
Duration
Purpose
Starting frame
Subject
Action
Environment
Camera shot
Camera angle
Camera movement
Lighting
Voice-over / dialogue
Sound
Visible text
Ending frame
Transition to next scene
Scene-specific continuity requirements

4. SCENE-BY-SCENE GENERATION PROMPTS
Provide one polished model-ready prompt for each scene.

5. AUDIO & VOICE-OVER TIMELINE
Map narration, dialogue, music and important sound effects to the scene sequence.

6. EDITING PLAN
Explain:

Scene order
Cut points
Transition types
Pacing

7. GLOBAL NEGATIVE / PROTECTION INSTRUCTIONS
List recurring generation failures to avoid.

8. CONTINUITY CHECKLIST
Verify:

Character
Clothing
Product
Food
Props
Location
Lighting
Object states
Camera direction
Audio

9. FINAL SEQUENCE CHECK
Confirm the complete video tells one coherent story.

IMPORTANT:
- Treat every scene as part of one production.
- Preserve character, product, location and object-state continuity.
- Do not create filler scenes.
- Match scene duration to the amount of action.
- Keep camera choices purposeful.
- Align visuals with narration.
- Prefer simple cuts over unnecessary transition effects.
- Do not invent claims, results, branding or social proof.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Build the story before the shots",
    description:
      "Define the hook, progression, payoff and ending first so each generated scene contributes to one clear video rather than becoming an isolated clip.",
  },
  {
    title: "Create a global continuity lock",
    description:
      "Fix character identity, clothing, products, props, locations, lighting and other recurring details before writing individual scene prompts.",
  },
  {
    title: "Plan every scene independently",
    description:
      "Give each scene its own duration, purpose, action, camera direction, first frame and final frame so it can be generated reliably.",
  },
  {
    title: "Plan the edit as well as the generation",
    description:
      "Define how clips connect, where cuts happen and how voice-over, sound and visual continuity carry across the complete sequence.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Platform: Facebook Reel.",
    "Total duration: 30 seconds.",
    "Format: 9:16.",
    "Topic: Simple caramel custard recipe.",
    "Structure: Finished dessert hook → caramel → custard mixture → steaming → unmoulding → final serving.",
    "Style: Premium photorealistic food-commercial.",
    "Environment: Same warm home kitchen throughout.",
  ],

  output: [
    "SCENE 1 — HOOK: Begin with the finished caramel custard in close-up as one spoon gently cuts through the smooth surface. Use this as the appetite-driving opening.",
    "SCENE 2 — CARAMEL: Show sugar and water becoming amber caramel in the same kitchen environment, then pouring into the round pan.",
    "SCENE 3 — CUSTARD: Show the milk mixture heating on the stove, followed by a controlled close-up of the prepared custard mixture being poured into the caramel-lined pan.",
    "SCENE 4 — STEAMING: Show the same round pan being placed inside the steamer, preserving the kitchen, cookware and visual style.",
    "SCENE 5 — UNMOULDING: Show the fully chilled custard being released onto a serving plate, keeping the dessert shape consistent.",
    "SCENE 6 — FINAL: Return to a premium close-up of the finished custard with one clean slice removed and caramel sauce flowing naturally.",
    "CONTINUITY: Same kitchen, pan style, dessert shape, colour grade and food identity across all related scenes. Do not allow the custard to change size, shape or texture unexpectedly.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Continuity includes object state",
    description:
      "If an ingredient is poured, a cake is cut or packaging is opened in one scene, the following scene should reflect that change instead of resetting the object.",
  },
  {
    title: "Generate scenes, then edit them together",
    description:
      "AI video models usually perform better when each scene is generated as a focused short clip and the complete story is assembled during editing.",
  },
  {
    title: "Let the camera evolve with the story",
    description:
      "Use wider shots for context and closer shots for important details instead of adding dramatic movement to every single scene.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Multi-Scene Video Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Plan coherent multi-scene AI videos with scene timing, character continuity, camera progression, transitions, voice-over and editing guidance."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="VIDEO GENERATION"
    title="Multi-Scene Video"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Turn a complete idea into a connected scene-by-scene AI video plan with consistent characters, products, locations, camera language and audio."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Multi-scene video",
      "Storyboard planning",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Recipe videos",
      "Product videos",
      "Reels & Shorts",
      "Commercials",
      "Educational videos",
      "Story-driven content",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Plan one production, not a collection of unrelated clips."
    introDescription="Lock continuity first, give every scene a clear purpose, control timing and camera progression, then generate each clip with an editing plan for the complete sequence."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a recipe into a connected six-scene Reel."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Continuity starts before the first clip is generated."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Only need one video shot?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Text-to-Video Prompt for a single scene, or Image-to-Video Prompt when you already have a reference image to animate."
    warningTitle="Individually good clips can still create a bad sequence"
    warningText="Review the complete edit for character drift, changing products, reset object states, inconsistent lighting, mismatched camera direction and narration timing. Continuity must be checked across scene boundaries, not only inside each generated clip."
  />
</BaseLayout>